ed inside the container via `btrfs device add` to make rootfs writable.\n  * **Partition 2 (10 GiB ext4)**: Dedicated for Cozo / RocksDB (`allow_fallocate = false` to preserve sparseness, bypassing Btrfs CoW overhead).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Repository Boundary\n* Container definitions were decoupled from the core odbus codebase and initialized in their own repo:\n  * **[`/srv/git/3tched-container`](file:///srv/git/3tched-container)**: Holds pure JSON instance templates (`chatbot-template.json`), Incus profiles (`3tched-lungs.json`), and the storage partition script (`make-cozo-device.sh`).","thinking":"The chatbot container serves as the template. Individual containers are differentiated by their UUID and WireGuard key/identity store (sled). This sled is associated with a specific entity, with a FUSE mount within containers and a socket incus mounted to the container alongside the root directory.\n\n\nDuring a conversation yesterday, October 26, 2024 (12:40 PM - 1:25 PM, session `952bc719-abd2-42eb-a14d-385b181c96ff`), it became clear that `/etc/identity/` contains mappings to subvolumes, but these aren't critical. Each configured identity resides separately, within a loop in the storage system, distinct from the shared baseline and upgrade image. A hybrid storage setup was specified: one partition on btrfs and another on ext4.\n\nProvisioning entails copying the hybrid device to storage, renaming the container (sequential ID), updating the hostname, starting the container, and adding the btrfs device; the btrfs backup would be in the template. The chatbot container should be its own repository, similar to the user interface. It was considered whether each bot should have its home directory or instance. The identity's backend will be shared, but the identity will be independently 3tched.\n\n\n"}
